# Sentry source-map upload. Build-time only.
#
# Copy this to `.env.sentry-build-plugin` (gitignored) and fill in all three.
# That is the name `vite.config.ts` loads, and it loads it rather than leaving
# it to the plugin because whether to emit source maps at all is decided while
# the config is being evaluated, before the plugin exists.
#
# With all three set, `yarn build` emits hidden source maps, uploads them, and
# deletes them again before `wrangler deploy` publishes `dist/`. With any of
# them missing the build still succeeds, prints a warning, and emits no maps
# at all — deliberately, since `wrangler.toml` serves everything in `dist/`
# and an unuploaded `.map` there is this app's source, published. Production
# stack traces from such a build are minified.
#
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN is a real secret — it grants write access to your Sentry
# org, unlike the public DSN in `.env.example`. Two rules follow:
#
#   - Use an ORGANIZATION auth token (Sentry -> organization settings -> auth
#     tokens), not a personal one, so uploads survive one person rotating
#     their credentials. It is shown once, at creation.
#   - Never give any of these a `VITE_` prefix. Vite inlines anything so
#     prefixed into the client bundle, and a copy of the token there is a
#     copy for every visitor.
#
# Anything already exported in the shell overrides this file, so CI can set
# the same three names as secrets and ignore it.

# SENTRY_ORG=
# SENTRY_PROJECT=
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=
